=== SIGNATURE ===
prng(seed, [lo], [hi], [n])

=== COMMENT ===
Non-volatile pseudorandom integers: a vertical list of n values in lo..hi inclusive (defaults 0, 100, 1). Same arguments always give the same list; each integer is equally likely. Fully vectorised (no recursion) so large n is fast - built for Monte Carlo.

=== CODE ===
=LAMBDA(seed, [lo], [hi], [n],
  LET(
    low, IF(ISOMITTED(lo), 0, INT(lo)),
    high, IF(ISOMITTED(hi), 100, INT(hi)),
    n_out, IF(ISOMITTED(n), 1, INT(n)),
    start, MIN(low, high),
    span, ABS(high - low) + 1,
    modulus, 4294967296,
    mulmod, LAMBDA(val, mult,
      MOD(val * MOD(mult, 65536) + MOD(val * INT(mult / 65536), 65536) * 65536, modulus)),
    xorshift, LAMBDA(val, shift, BITXOR(val, BITRSHIFT(val, shift))),
    mix, LAMBDA(val, LET(
      step_a, xorshift(val, 16),
      step_b, mulmod(step_a, 2146121005),
      step_c, xorshift(step_b, 15),
      step_d, mulmod(step_c, 2221713035),
      xorshift(step_d, 16))),
    seed_mix, mix(MOD(INT(seed), modulus)),
    draw_ix, IF(n_out = 1, 1, SEQUENCE(n_out)),
    keys, MOD(seed_mix + mulmod(draw_ix, 2654435761), modulus),
    start + MOD(mix(keys), span)
  )
)

=== DESCRIPTION ===
Deterministic, non-volatile pseudorandom integers for simulation work.

Arguments:
  seed  - any integer. The same seed (with the same lo/hi/n) always produces the same list; different seeds give unrelated lists, even consecutive ones (1, 2, 3...).
  [lo]  - lowest value, inclusive. Default 0.
  [hi]  - highest value, inclusive. Default 100. (If lo > hi they are swapped.)
  [n]   - how many numbers to return, 1 or more. Default 1.

Returns:
  A vertical array of n integers, each drawn independently and (near-)uniformly from lo..hi. With n = 1 the result is a true scalar (not a 1x1 array), so it is safe inside SEQUENCE, MAKEARRAY etc.

How it works:
  Each output is a 32-bit integer hash of (seed, position) - splitmix style: the seed is hashed, a golden-ratio Weyl step is added per position, and the result is run through a 3-round xorshift-multiply finaliser (lowbias32). All arithmetic stays below 2^53 (products are split at 16 bits) so it is bit-exact in Excel doubles. The 32-bit value is reduced with MOD to lo..hi; the bias from that is under span / 4.3 billion, i.e. negligible.

Because it is stateless (no SCAN/REDUCE), the whole list is built with array arithmetic in one pass, so n of 100,000+ is fast. Unlike RAND/RANDBETWEEN/RANDARRAY it never recalculates on its own - results only change if the arguments change. Nothing else in the workbook (sheet, cell, time) affects it.

Monte Carlo tips:
  - One trial = one seed: MAP(SEQUENCE(trials), LAMBDA(t, <stat of prng(t, lo, hi, draws)>)). Use a different seed per trial, not slices of one long list.
  - Or draw one long list prng(seed, lo, hi, trials*draws) and reshape it with WRAPROWS.
  - Need a random real in [0,1)? prng(seed, 0, 999999, n) / 1000000.

Examples:
  prng(7)                 ->  one integer 0..100
  prng(7, 1, 6, 10)       ->  ten dice rolls (always the same ten for seed 7)
  prng(42, 1, 6, 10000)   ->  ten thousand dice rolls, roughly 1/6 each face
